T1  - The influence of socioeconomic status, parents, peers, psychological needs, and task values on middle school student motivation for school music ensemble participation.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Holster, Jacob D

Y1  - 2022//
N2  - There are a multitude of influences on adolescents' decisions to enroll in elective instrumental music ensembles. While some music teachers might rely on external rewards such as end-of-year trips to encourage retention from year to year, and some music education researchers have posited that previous experience is the best predictor of future enrollment, middle-school students' sociocultural and psychological characteristics may best account for their intentions to continue in band or orchestra. In this study, I examined task values from Expectancy-Value Theory (Eccles et al., 1983), and needs satisfaction variables from Self-Determination Theory (Deci & Ryan, 1985), alongside environmental factors such as parent and peer influence as well as socioeconomic status, in an effort to explain variance in seventh- and eighth-grade instrumental students' elective intentions. More specifically, the purpose of this study was to replicate and extend Freer & Evans' (2018) model of elective intentions.I distributed a questionnaire to a sample of seventh- and eighth-grade instrumental music students in a large, highly-diverse school district in the Southeastern United States. Participants (n = 42) responded to items regarding socioeconomic status, peer influence, parent influence, needs satisfaction (i.e. autonomy, competence, and relatedness), task values (i.e., interest, utility, attainment, and cost), and elective intentions. Reliability estimates were used to ensure that all responses to each subscale were internally consistent, where autonomy was the only subscale that did not achieve a sufficient Cronbach's alpha level of .70. Data were then analyzed to assess normality, multicollinearity, and missing responses before structural equation modeling (SEM) was utilized to test relationships among the variables.The resultant structural equation model met established fit thresholds, with several interesting predictive relationships. Elective intentions were positively predicted by task values (beta = .72) and socioeconomic status (beta = .29), and negatively predicted by needs satisfaction (beta = -.27). Task values were predicted by needs satisfaction (beta = .46), parent influence (beta = .23), and peer influence (beta = .27). Needs satisfaction was predicted by parent influence alone (beta = .53). The model explained 27% of variance in needs satisfaction, 49% of variance in task values, and 44% of variance in elective intentions.According to these results, parents exert a large amount of influence over the extent to which participants' needs were satisfied, and a small amount of influence over the task values of their children. Additionally, task values appeared to mediate the negative influence of needs satisfaction on elective intentions. In essence, when parents and teachers were supportive of students' autonomy, competence, and relatedness, then instrumental music learning tasks were valued. Task values, in turn, predicted middle school instrumental students' intentions to persist and enroll in future instrumental music courses. These results have implications for peers, parents, and instrumental music teachers. Additionally, reliable scales for the self-report measurement of adolescents' perceptions of peer influence and socioeconomic status were utilized, linking lower socioeconomic student backgrounds to lower elective intentions. The implications of the COVID-19 pandemic on the present study were also discussed. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
