T1  - Characterizing Complex Alleles of ABCA4 in Inherited Retinal Degeneration
T3  - Association for Research in Vision and Ophthalmology Annual Meeting, ARVO 2025. Salt Lake City, UT United States.
A1  - Sadeghi M.
A1  - Jones J.
A1  - Biswas S.
A1  - Biswas-Fiss E.E.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Purpose : Complex alleles-comprising two or more variants in cis-are well-known contributors to the severity and diversity of diseases. The purpose of this study is to investigate the phenotypic effects of missense complex alleles in the ABCA4gene, which are associated with inherited retinal degenerations, including Stargardt disease (STGD1). To elucidate their distinct contributions, we evaluated each variant within these complex alleles individually and in combination, using both in silico and in vitro approaches. Methods : Six ABCA4 complex alleles identified in individuals with retinal dystrophies were analyzed, based on reports in the scientific literature. The structural and functional impacts of the variants were assessed individually and in their ciscombinations. Structural analyses were performed using AlphaFold2 models and available experimental structures of the human ABCA4 protein. A virus-like particle (VLP)-based system was employed to express ABCA4 variants, enabling comparisons with the wild-type (WT) protein in terms of expression level, surface localization, and basal and retinal-stimulated ATPase activities. These results were compared with patient clinical presentations and individual REVEL pathogenicity predictions to evaluate the potential synergistic effects of variants in cis. Results : Clear genotype-phenotype relationships were observed when examining the variants as components of a complex allele. The stability and function of the ABCA4 protein were differentially affected by individual variants, with cumulative impacts observed in the context of complex alleles. Structural and functional analyses of cis variants revealed synergistic effects that differed from the pathogenicity predictions of individual variants by the REVEL ensemble method. Conclusions : Evaluating the individual components of complex alleles in ABCA4 provides a deeper understanding of their pathogenic mechanisms, which is critical for accurately determining their contributions to disease phenotypes. Our findings underscore the importance of considering the context of complex alleles when assessing genetic variants.
