T1  - Generalized metabolic avatar: Integrating biohacking into precision nutrition for personalized weight management and environmental sustainability
T3  - 46th ESPEN Congress on Clinical Nutrition & Metabolism. Milan Italy.
A1  - Abeltino A.
A1  - Serantoni C.
A1  - Riente A.
A1  - De Giulio M.M.
A1  - De Spirito M.
A1  - Maulucci G.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Rationale: This study introduces an innovative approach to combat the global obesity epidemic by integrating precision nutrition and biohacking methodologies. Recognizing the multifaceted nature of individual metabolic systems influenced by genetic, environmental, and lifestyle factors, the study presents the Generalized Metabolic Avatar (GMA) as a versatile framework for personalized dietary interventions. Method(s): The GMA was developed through ensemble regression models. Comparative analysis with the Personalized Metabolic Avatar (PMA) [1,2] served as the gold-standard for evaluating prediction accuracy, simulation capability, and computational efficiency. Additionally, a pilot study with 4 participants assessed a biohacking-based diet generator's effectiveness in personalized nutrition while reducing caloric intake and environmental impact. Result(s): The GMA accurately predicted weight changes and metabolic responses, offering tailored strategies for health enhancement. Comparative analysis with the PMA showed similar performance (PMA RMSE: 0.42+/-0.04; GMA RMSE: 0.44+/-0.17) and reduced computational time (PMA: 12.0+/-1.22 s; GMA: 0.15+/-0.11 s). Leveraging diverse datasets, the GMA demonstrated substantial potential in precision nutrition, addressing eating disorders and obesity. Preliminary pilot study results supported effective digital biohacking recommendations by the GMA, leading to significant reductions in calorie intake (14.24%) and carbon footprint impact (15.12%) for sustainable weight loss. Conclusion(s): This approach offers a promising solution for combating obesity by considering individual preferences and environmental sustainability within personalized nutrition methods. References: [1] https://doi.org/10.3390/nu14173520 [2] https://doi.org/10.3390/nu15051199 Disclosure of Interest: None declaredCopyright © 2024
