T1  - Covid-19 induced data shift causes degradation of predictive model performance in perioperative medicine
T3  - International Conference on Complex Acute Illness (ICCAI) 2023. Augsburg Germany.
A1  - Kagerbauer S.M.
A1  - Ulm B.
A1  - Podtschaske A.
A1  - Blobner M.
A1  - Jungwirth B.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Objectives: Clinical medicine has to be regarded as a non-stationary environment1 with many influences impairing the predictive capabilities of machine learning (ML) algorithms. In this study, we investigated the impact of Covid-19-induced changes on the performance of ML models in the perioperative setting. Method(s): Our dataset included 109719 surgical procedures performed at a German university hospital with which we created models to predict postoperative in-hospital mortality from pre-operatively available data. The training- and test datasets ranged from June 2014 - December 2019. Validation was performed with data from January - March 2020 (pre-pandemic) and from April - May 2020 (in-pandemic). Deep Learning, Distributed Random Forest, Gradient Boosting Machine (GBM), XGBoost, a Generalized Linear Model and Stacked Ensemble Models (aka "Super Learner") were trained. The impact of pandemic-related changes on model performance was quantified by area under receiver operating characteristic (AUROC) and area under precision-recall (AUPR) curve. Changes in the probability distribution of the input variables before and during the pandemic were analyzed by Komolgorov-Smirnov test. Result(s): In the pre-pandemic, all models showed good AUROCs (0.86-0.95) whereas AUPRs ranged between 0.14 and 0.26. Validating the models in the pandemic dataset resulted in AUROCs between 0.81-0.91 and a drastic decline in AUPRs to 0.05 - 0.09. These changes were statistically significant in Deep Learning, GBM, XGBoost and Stacked Ensemble models. Out of 2803 parameters used, only 8 were commonly used in all models. Komolgorov-Smirnov-test between the test dataset and the in-pandemic dataset revealed statistically significant differences in parameter distribution2. Conclusion(s): Covid-19-related changes in the medical environment cause an impairment of model performance. This is most likely due to a change in the probability distribution of relevant input variables. Further studies are needed to create robust models and to develop early warning systems for a data shift taking place. Literatures: 1. Sugiyama M, Kawanabe K. Machine Learning in Non-Stationary Environments Introduction to Covariate Shift Adaptation. The MIT Press, 2012 2. Authenrieth M, van Dyk DA, Trotta R, Stenning DC. Stratified Learning: A GeneralPurpose Statistical Method for Improved Learning under Covariate Shift. arXiv 2023Copyright © 2024
