T1  - Prediction of Progression to Critical Illness in Heart Failure Patients Hospitalized with COVID-19: Multiple Chronic Conditions Matter
T3  - International Conferenceof the American Thoracic Society, ATS 2022. San Francisco, CA United States.
A1  - Burns K.
A1  - Walo R.
A1  - Patel D.
A1  - Whiteside Z.
A1  - Malik B.
A1  - Thompson R.
A1  - Baez Rodriguez K.
A1  - Villasmil R.
A1  - Abdelal Q.
A1  - Hamad K.
A1  - Heibel R.
A1  - Wiese-Rometsch W.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - RATIONALE: Heart failure (HF) patients hospitalized with COVID-19 demonstrate increased risk for progression to critical illness (PCI, ICU admission or death) with excess death rate amplified by concurrent multiple chronic conditions (MCC). We reasoned that pathogenicity inferred from elevations in putative COVID-19 blood-based biomarkers is not sufficient to comprehensively assess presentation risk of PCI. Bootstrap Forest (BF) modeling was used to compare the predictive value of biomarkers with versus without MCC versus PCI during incident hospitalization. METHOD(S): Discharge electronic medical records with IRB exemption underwent an extraction of presentation demographics, anthropometrics, laboratory results (within 8h) and ICD-10-CMbased Elixhauser comorbidity categories (AHRQ, v2021.1). Univariate logistic regression identified intergroup differences (p<.05) in MCC versus PCI and provided a receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve for each laboratory assay versus probability of PCI to compute Youden's cut point reflecting best tradeoff between sensitivity and specificity. BF aggregation optimized area under ROC curve (AUROC) representing multivariate prediction accuracy while providing explained variance proportion (EV%) contributed by each variable. Continuous data summarized with median [IQR] were compared using Kruskal-Wallis test. Discrete data summarized as proportions were compared with chi-squared test. RESULT(S): Among 2153 consecutive patients discharged from index COVID-19 hospitalization alive or expired between March 14, 2020, and June 30, 2021, 309 with HF were studied. Progression-free (n=202, 65%) and PCI (n=107, 35%) demographics were statistically equivalent including males (69% vs. 63%) with sex pooled age 77[69-86] vs. 75[63-84] years and BMI 28[24-33] vs. 31[26-36] kg/m2 distributed across whites (80% vs. 78%), blacks (13% vs. 15%), and other races (7% vs. 7%). There were no intergroup differences in ACE2 inhibitor, statin, metformin, steroid, or HF treatments at admission. Progression-free versus PCI prevalent comorbidities included diabetes (36% vs. 48%, p=.03), obesity (31% vs. 48%, p<.003), deficiency anemias (19% vs. 44%, p<.0001), and chronic coagulation disorder (18% vs 28%, p=.03). Biomarker cut points ensemble was 84% predictive (AUROC, Figure 1A) and when combined with MCC, prediction improved to 91% with more stable performance (Figure 1B). CONCLUSION(S): Our training model demonstrates a machine learning strategy to leverage synergy of integrating MCC with presentation biomarkers to enhance the understanding of host response to SARS-CoV-2 infection contributing to risk for PCI in hospitalized HF patients. Although we evince potential value of integrating most prevalent MCC pattern, an automated approach can be developed within the EMR to temporally refine risk stratification by using updated biomarker data along with patient specific MCC.
