T1  - Electronic Pediatric Lung Sounds Classification Using a Machine Learning Approach
T3  - 21st International Congress of Pediatric Pulmonology. Online.
A1  - Kim K.
A1  - Park J.S.
A1  - Kim K.S.
A1  - Suh D.I.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Background: Auscultation is a simple, non-invasive, and radiation-free method of screening respiratory diseases. While auscultation by a conventional stethoscope requires a person-toperson encounter, subject to interpretation, electronic stethoscopes enable lung sounds to be recorded and shared for second opinion. During this pandemic era, the need for a prompt and precise screening tool for respiratory disorders can be met by an automated classification tool of electronically collected lung sounds. Objective(s): We aimed to develop and evaluate a machine learning model to classify pediatric electronic respiratory sounds. Method(s): Lung sounds were recorded during routine physical examination at Seoul National University Children's Hospital Pediatric Pulmonology outpatient clinic using a digital stethoscope (ThinkLabs One Digital Stethoscope, Centennial, CO, USA) connected to a wired audio recorder (PCM-A10, Sony, Tokyo, Japan) from July 2019 to November 2019. Recorded lung sounds were edited into short clips containing 1 or 2 breath cycles and labeled into 3 classes: normal, crackle, or wheezing. Based on Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients (MFCCs) extracted from the sound clips, lung sounds were visualized using uniform manifold approximation and projection (UMAP), a nonlinear dimensionality reduction technique. MFCCs repeated and cropped into 6-seconds were used as inputs to train 4 classification tasks: 1) normal vs. abnormal, 2) normal vs. crackle, 3) normal vs. wheezing, and 4) crackle vs. wheezing. Ensemble SVM classifiers were trained using K-fold cross-validation (K=10). The model was evaluated using a prospectively collected validation dataset (June 2021 to July 2021) and compared with classification performances of 5 pediatric infection and pulmonology specialists and 5 non-pediatricians. Result(s): A total of 680 clips (288 normal, 200 crackle, and 192 wheezing) were included in the training and internal validation set. The 2D plot using UMAP yielded a clear clustering of the 3 classes. The model's accuracy of the internal validation for tasks 1) normal vs. abnormal, 2) normal vs. crackle, 3) normal vs. wheezing, and 4) crackle vs. wheezing were 83.68%, 83.67%, 80.94%, and 90.42%, respectively. Accuracy of the prospective validation set of 90 clips (28 normal, 31 crackle, and 31 wheezing) for the 4 tasks were 82.22%, 67.74%, 67.80%, and 81.36%, respectively. The average pediatrician performances for the 4 tasks were 84.89%, 77.74%, 78.64%, and 84.41%, respectively, and the average non-pediatrician performances 80.67%, 66.13%, 73.90%, and 76.27%. Conclusion(s): We derived and evaluated a machine learning model to classify pediatric electronic lung sounds with close performance to pediatric specialists and higher performance than non-pediatric clinicians. Artificial intelligence-assisted pediatric auscultation may be of great value in this pandemic era where the need for accurate and efficient screening of respiratory diseases is vital. With further improved accuracy from a larger dataset, this model would enhance the diagnostic process in pediatric pulmonology.
