T1  - A Blood Inventory Forecasting Model Developed in Response to the Blood Shortage Seen during the COVID-19 Pandemic
T3  - American Association of Blood Banks Annual Meeting, AABB 2022. Orlando, FL United States.
A1  - Hasan H.Y.
A1  - DesRochers L.
A1  - Fritsch C.
A1  - Pehler A.
A1  - Weiss J.
A1  - Connor J.
A1  - Raife T.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Background/Case Studies: The US faced the most severe blood supply shortage in over a decade during the COVID- 19 pandemic. In December 2021, our blood supplier implemented an allocation plan, based on 3 years of historic blood usage, to distribute the limited supply to hospital customers. In response, our health care system developed a real-time blood inventory forecasting model to assist hospital administration, clinical services, and the transfusion service in making blood management decisions. Study Design/Methods: An automated report was developed to forecast type O red blood cell (RBC) usage. The forecast used 3 data sources (Table): the current blood inventory at 3:30 am (2nd column), the expected allocation from our blood supplier (3rd column), and previous transfusion orders placed (5th column). To predict blood usage (4th column), we trained a model to forecast this usage. For historical usage, we used 1 previous year of completed transfusion orders, split into 10 months of historic data used for training the models, and 2 months for testing the trained models. Data points outside 2 standard deviations were removed. Multiple models were trained and tested. The chosen model was a time series model (tbats) based on Root Mean Square Error. The model is used to project inventory for 2 weeks within 90% confidence intervals (CIs). Results/Findings: The table shows a sample 4-day period of blood use and subsequent 4-day forecast. The report includes bar graphs that update daily and line graphs that update on Fridays after receipt of new allocation data. For example, from the table, the model predicts that on 4/2 8 units of O+ RBCs will be used, and 10 units will be received. If the model had predicted 30 units would be used (while receiving 10 units), the line graph projection would approach the minimum target inventory value, alerting personnel to monitor the current inventory and prepare to implement critical resource management measures. Management measures were developed in a separate plan, including a detailed communication cascade, a triage team, potential elective surgery postponement, and potential blood inventory augmentation from other blood suppliers or hospitals. Conclusion(s): An automated, real-time blood forecasting tool was developed and successfully used by hospital personnel to guide critical resource management decisions during a serious blood shortage.
