T1  - COVID-19 Patients Expressed Distinct Clinical Trait Signatures at Index Hospitalization Across the Four SARS-CoV-2 Pandemic Waves in Florida
T3  - International Conferenceof the American Thoracic Society, ATS 2022. San Francisco, CA United States.
A1  - Sia J.
A1  - Idogun P.
A1  - Patel D.
A1  - Motie I.
A1  - Hamad K.
A1  - Geary M.
A1  - Seaman J.
A1  - Wiese-Rometsch W.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - RATIONALE: Risks have been associated with COVID-19 mortality, but predictive stability across four pandemic surges has not been reported. Machine learning identified presentation traits at index hospitalization independently associated with mortality. We compared explained variance (EV%) by each trait during pandemic Wave 1: March 10, 2020 - May 31, 2020; Wave 2: June 1, 2020 - Sept 30, 2020; Wave 3: Oct 1, 2020 - June 18, 2021; and Wave 4: June 19, 2021 - Sept 30, 2021 at an 818-bed academic safety-net hospital. METHOD(S): Demographics, physiology, laboratory results, ICD-10-CM-based comorbidity, COVID- 19 directed treatment and administrative data were extracted under IRB exemption from electronic medical records. Generalized regression with adaptive LASSO identified traits associated with mortality in at least one of the waves controlling for COVID-19 directed treatment. Univariate logistic regression for each trait created a within-wave receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve with optimal cut-point (Youden Index) associated with mortality. Bootstrap Forest (BF) computed within-wave respectively area under ROC (AUROC) representing multi-trait prediction accuracy and EV% for each trait. Continuous data summarized with median [IQR] were compared using Kruskal-Wallis. Discrete data summarized as proportions were compared with chi-square. RESULT(S): Patients (4430) were distributed across Wave 1 (145, 3%), Wave 2 (664, 15%), Wave 3 (1908, 43%) and Wave 4 (1713, 39%) with respective mortality of 14%, 9%, 8%, and 12% (p<.0001); COVID-19 directed treatments included enoxaparin (prophylactic and/or therapeutic) (72%, 76%, 69%, 78%; p<.0001), dexamethasone (3%, 64%, 62%, 76%; p<.0001), remdesivir (10%, 35%, 32%, 45%; p<.0001), tocilizumab (15%, 3%, 5%, 7%; p<.0001), baricitinib (0%, 0%, 0%, 16%; p<.0001); intubation rate (20%, 9%, 8%, 10%, p=.0002); time(h) ED-presentation to intubation (29[3-122], 96[27-191], 109[34-201], 119[38-213], p<.0001); ICU admission rate (28%, 22%, 17%, 17%; p=.002); time(h) ED-presentation to ICU admission (26[6-76], 12[5-110], 36[7-114], 49[9-213], p=.0005); mechanical ventilation days (11[9-24], 10[4-17], 5[2-14], 8[4-16], p=.001). Figure 1 illustrates inter-wave variation in trait cut-points and EV% with relatively stable ensemble AUROC. Lactate dehydrogenase or LDH/albumin ratio consistently exhibited greatest EV%. Cumulative mortality risk (EV18%) associated with obesity, hypothyroidism, hypertension, deficiency anemia and chronic coagulation disease in Wave 1 is EV0% in Wave 4 reflecting median age reduction. CONCLUSION(S): Our longitudinal training models suggest that COVID-19 presentation risk tools may not generalize across pandemic surges. Caveat for applying our data includes monocenter study with many patients self-reporting vaccination status. Study strength includes demonstrating utility of longitudinal machine modeling as a tool for learning healthcare system science. (Figure Presented).
