T1  - Neural circuit mechanisms underlying stress-induced alcohol seeking behavior
T3  - 42nd Annual Scientific Meeting of the Research Society on Alcoholism, RSA 2019. Minneapolis, MN United States.
A1  - Suh J.
A1  - Silveri M.M.
A1  - Ressler K.J.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - Alcohol is the most commonly abused substance in individuals seeking treatment for post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). Despite the high rates of comorbidity between PTSD and AUD, there is a substantial gap in understanding how traumatic experiences lead to a transition from initially controlled alcohol consumption to the development of alcohol seeking and abuse. It has been shown that individuals with comorbid AUD and PTSD exhibit hyper-reactivity of the amygdala upon presentation of both aversive stimuli and alcohol cues. These intriguing findings suggest that the amygdala is a key structure that mediates interactions between AUD and PTSD; however, neural circuit mechanisms underlying amygdala dysfunction in AUD and PTSD comorbidity are not well understood. Recently, studies in mice have indicated that distinct subpopulations of neurons within the amygdala are differentially responsible for the activation and inhibition of fear memory, and divergent ensemble activity from these subpopulations seems to mediate positive or negative valence coding. Specifically, we have found that a specific excitatory neuronal population in the basolateral amygdala (BLA), marked by Thy1 expression (Thy1 + ), serve as 'Fear-Off' neurons. These neurons also heavily project to the nucleus accumbens (NAcc), a core structure for reward-based learning and substance addiction, suggesting that these neurons directly inhibit fear and may support appetitive behavior. To address this issue, a well-established immobilization stress (IMO) procedure and conditioned place preference (CPP) paradigm with alcohol (EtOH, 2 g/kg BW, i.p.) were employed in mice. Unstressed mice were found to display a significant preference for the chamber associated with EtOH, but then rapid extinction of the preference without reinforcer. Notably, mice with prior IMO stress showed significantly enhanced alcohol-chamber association and reduced CPP extinction. A circuit-basedmanipulation was then performed in Thy1 + neurons using Thy1-Cre driver mice and adeno-associated virus encoding Cre-dependent halorhodopsin. Optogenetic inhibition at Thy1 + cell bodies in the BLA or Thy1 + projections within the NAcc was found to significantly disrupt EtOH-CPP formation. The findings suggest that alcohol exposure alters the firing activity of subpopulations of amygdala neurons and the functional interactions between the BLA and NAcc via its projections. A prior traumatic experience may augment these changes and lead to enhanced alcohol seeking behavior.
