T1  - Prediction of Bacteremia based on 12-year medical data using machine learning approach
T3  - 4th International Interscience Conference of Infection and Chemotherapy and 12th International Symposium on Antimicrobial Agents and Resistance, ICIC - ISAAR 2019. Gyeongju South Korea.
A1  - Lee K.H.
A1  - Dong J.J.
A1  - Jeong S.J.
A1  - Chae M.-H.
A1  - Lee B.S.
A1  - Kim H.J.
A1  - Ko S.H.
A1  - Song Y.G.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - Backgrounds: Analysis using artificial intelligence techniques have recently been attracting attention, and they are mainly used for diagnosis using medical image or predicting the prognosis of chronic diseases such as malignancies. The application of the machine learning approach to the acute infectious disease is not yet usable. The aim of this study is to make a model of predicting bacteremia when the blood culture is applied to the patients of acute infectious diseases. Method(s): From January 2007 to December 2018, all blood culture episodes were extracted from the electrical medical record of patients over eighteen years old at Sinchon and Gangnam Severance Hospital, a university-affiliated tertiary-care center in Seoul. Only significant true bacteremia were included except contamination of normal flora and fungal infection. Patients with the result of same bacterial strain within 72 hours were excluded. The gender, age, vital sign, and the results of laboratory test within 24 hours from onset time of blood culture were used as analytical variables. For data training, the multi-layer perceptron (MLP), stacking ensemble and xgboost method were used, and then validation was performed. Result(s): A total of 38,752 bacteraemia episodes were identified from 622,771 patients. In MLP, with 128 hidden layer nodes, the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) of the prediction performance was 0.752 (95% CI; 0.750-0.751) and in MLP with 64-128-64 hidden layer nodes, it was 0.742 (95% CI; 0.732-0.751). In stacking ensemble method, the AUC was 0.751 (95% CI; 0.748-0.753). The xgboost, another ensemble model was used for analysis, the AUC was non inferior compared with other machine learning method (AUC; 0.751 [95%CI 0.745-0.757]). Conclusion(s): Prediction of bacteremia using artificial intelligence showed high AUC, and the possibility of applying a machine learning approach to prediction of prognosis in acute infectious diseases. Based on several important vital signs and the results of blood tests, the real-time monitoring will be helpful in improving clinical prognosis in real clinical practice. (Table Presented).
