T1  - The contribution of heterosexual anal intercourse (AI) to HIV transmission in Western Cape and KwaZulu-Natal Provinces in South Africa
T3  - 2nd HIV Research for Prevention Conference, HIVR4P 2016. Chicago, IL United States.
A1  - Silhol R.
A1  - Owen B.
A1  - Elmes J.
A1  - Shacklett B.
A1  - Van der Straten A.
A1  - Dimitrov D.
A1  - McGowan I.
A1  - Boily M.-C.
Y1  - 2016//
N2  - Background: Receptive AI (RAI) increases HIV acquisition risk compared to vaginal intercourse (VI) and may greatly contribute to heterosexual HIV epidemics. Based on a systematic review of heterosexual AI practices in South Africa (SA), we estimated the contribution of AI to heterosexual HIV transmission in Western Cape (WC) and KwaZulu-Natal (KZN). Method(s): An HIV transmission model representing AI practices was calibrated to each province's epidemic. Our review and published literature informed behavioural, biological and intervention parameters (e.g. AI and VI frequencies, condom use, and ART coverage). We identified multiple model fits matching key epidemiological data in each province over time (e.g. HIV prevalence and lifetime AI prevalence). The population attributable fraction of all HIV infections (among men & women) due to AI between 2010-2015 (PAF5) was estimated by comparing the number of new infections in scenarios where AI was assumed: (i) to increase HIV risk during RAI (RRrai=2-20-fold & RRiai=1-2- fold for insertive AI), (ii) not to increase HIV risk (RRrai=RRiai=1). We report the median & 10%-90% uncertainty interval (UI). Result(s): Our review suggests 5%-17% of heterosexual adults practiced AI in WC in face-to-face interviews (FTFI). Whereas in KZN 1%-6% or 15-20% of heterosexuals practiced AI if FTFI or if audio computer assisted (ACASI) interview methods were used, respectively. The predicted PAF5 was ~22% (UI:13-37%) in WC. In KZN, it was ~9% (UI:6-15%) for FTFI and ~30% (UI:23-40%) for ACASI AI ranges. PAF5 was up to 2-fold higher among female sex-workers, who practiced AI more often, and slightly lower for males than females not engaging in commercial sex. Conclusion(s): AI may contribute significantly to the HIV epidemic among heterosexual women and men in South Africa, especially if relying on AI estimates from more confidential interview methods such as ACASI. It should be openly addressed as a risk factor for heterosexual HIV transmission in public health interventions in South Africa.
