T1  - CCR5: Structure prediction, ligand docking, and mutagenesis validation aimed at disrupting HIV entry
T3  - 24th Annual Symposium of the Protein Society. San Diego, CA United States.
A1  - Goddard III W.
A1  - Trzaskowski B.
A1  - Abrol R.
Y1  - 2010//
N2  - G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) are intergral membrane proteins that mediate a large number of signal transduction pathways critical for biological function. The chemokine receptors CCR5 and CXCR4 are GPCRs that are the two major coreceptors for HIV entry into cells. The viral protein gp120 interacts with CD4 on the host cell surface and undergoes a conformational change that enables it to interact with the extracellular portions of CCR5 and CXCR4. Maraviroc is the only approved allosteric inhibitor of gp120 interaction with CCR5 but there are several others in clinical trials. These drugs bind to the transmembrane (TM) region of CCR5 and indirectly modulate the conformations of extracellular loops and N-terminus of CCR5. The virus mutates gp120 rapidly to overcome this indirect modulation as evidenced by the appearance of Maraviroc-resistant viral strains in the drug-taking population. Our strategy is to design a drug that directly engages the extracellular regions of CCR5 which we hope might permanently block the interaction of CCR5 with gp120. With this motivation we have predicted the structure of the human CCR5 receptor using our recently developed GEnSeMBLE method for GPCR structure prediction that generates the ensemble of low energy receptor conformations, which we expect to include all structures that play physiological roles in the functional and ligand binding properties of the receptor. We find that various known ligands select different receptor conformations among the nine lowest apo protein packings consistent with experiments on other GPCRs. We also find that to reliably predict the effect of mutations on ligand binding, it is necessary to consider the top 50 of the apo protein structures in order to capture the most stable ones for each particular mutant and its apo and ligand-bound forms. We will illustrate these results with recent experiments aimed at validating the computational predictions.
