A1  - Waters K.M.
A1  - Smith R.D.
A1  - Self S.
A1  - Katze M.G.
Y1  - 2010//
N2  - The systems biology approach to infectious disease research strives to achieve a global perspective of the interplay between host and pathogen. A key aspect of the approach is the use of high-throughput technologies for collecting quantitative data from multiple model systems for down-stream computational modeling efforts aimed at predicting system-wide behaviour to a pathogen. We are actively using this approach to study the virus-host interactions during HCV infection and pathogenesis. Our previous studies include genomic, proteomic and lipidomic analyses of HCV-infected Huh7.5 cells which revealed profound modifications in the transcriptome and proteome, including significant "metabolic reprogramming" that likely contributes to disease progression. Using integrative computational modeling techniques we further identified mitochondrial fatty acid oxidation enzymes as key cellular proteins targeted by HCV during this metabolic reprogramming and, have since confirmed the importance of these proteins to the viral life cycle via gene silencing and pharmacologic inhibition. Here we describe an extension of these activities to the analysis of serial liver biopsies acquired from HCV infected patients after liver transplantation. Comprehensive statistical characterization of this cohort identified clinical covariates significantly associated with outcome (progression to fibrosis or development of clinical cirrhosis) that were then used to build a predictive model (via logistic regression) identifying genes significantly associated with that outcome. The resulting genes map to numerous functional categories whose importance to HCV pathogenesis was predicted from our earlier studies of HCV-infection in vitro (e.g., cell cycle, cell growth and proliferation, cell death, and sphingolipid/ ceramide metabolism). Similarly, the results of our clinical proteome analyses and modeling efforts recapitulate our original predictions regarding the importance of fatty acid oxidation enzymes in HCV infection and identify additional candidate (antioxidant) proteins predicted to function as master regulators of HCV-associated liver disease progression. We further demonstrate that a subset of these changes occur prior to histological evidence of significant fibrosis, including numerous proteins that have been detected in blood. These findings provide new insights into the role of oxidative stress and hepatic mitochondrial dysfunction in HCV pathogenesis and, identify novel therapeutic targets and blood-based biomarker candidates of HCV-associated liver disease progression.
