T1  - Forecasting influenza with the Long Short-Term Memory Model: results from the 2023-2024 influenza season.

A1  - Cherukuri, Sneha P.
A1  - Bova, Mark L.
A1  - Mehta, Shaylee P.
A1  - Bautista, Christian T.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - This study evaluated the performance of the Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) model for forecasting influenza encounters among U.S. military service members, aiming to enhance early warning systems. The LSTM model, a type of recurrent neural network, was tested alongside other models like ARIMA, ETS, and VAR during the 2023-2024 influenza season. The LSTM model demonstrated the lowest median log-transformed Weighted Interval Score (WIS) across all forecasting horizons, indicating superior accuracy in predicting influenza encounters. However, neither the LSTM nor the ENSEMBLE models accurately predicted the peak influenza period in late December 2023, possibly due to recent shifts in seasonal influenza patterns post-COVID-19. The study highlights the potential of integrating LSTM into the ENSEMBLE model to improve influenza forecasting, although further optimization and validation are needed to enhance its performance and generalizability to other respiratory diseases.
